## Lessons Learned

### Symlinks Create Coupling

The shared lua-scripts directory was meant to share code between projects. In practice, it created:
- Fear of breaking other projects
- Merge conflicts when multiple projects evolve
- "Which version is this?" confusion

Duplication is better than coupling for independent projects.

### Git History as Documentation

The 119 commits in custom/.git had context like:
- "Fixed traveler spawn when no terrain found"
- "Reduced ambush interval to make game harder"
- "Added merchant NPC templates"

We lost that by snapshotting. But we gained:
- Clean slate for new history
- Issues as primary documentation
- LLM transcripts for detailed context

Trade-off: Historical context vs fresh start. We chose fresh start.

### Reference Implementation

The original wow-chat-1 scripts are preserved at `libs/wow-chat-1/` as a reference. When questions arise like "how did traveler spawning work before?" we can diff against the reference.

This enables:
- Verify bug fixes didn't regress
- Compare performance of old vs new approach
- Understand the evolution of ideas

